1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,758
Sweetheart, Christmas of two thousand twenty-two,

2
00:00:03,758 --> 00:00:06,674
my friend Dottie walked into a mansion

3
00:00:06,674 --> 00:00:08,975
on Lake Erie with a little box

4
00:00:08,975 --> 00:00:12,120
wrapped in brown paper and kitchen twine.

5
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:14,573
A gift for her granddaughter and her

6
00:00:14,573 --> 00:00:15,331
new husband.

7
00:00:15,511 --> 00:00:17,882
And in front of forty guests,

8
00:00:17,882 --> 00:00:20,581
the groom's mother held it up and

9
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:22,216
said, oh, how sweet,

10
00:00:22,216 --> 00:00:24,506
did you wrap it in a grocery

11
00:00:24,506 --> 00:00:24,833
bag?

12
00:00:25,013 --> 00:00:26,441
Everybody laughed.

13
00:00:26,621 --> 00:00:27,990
Two hours later,

14
00:00:27,990 --> 00:00:30,813
nobody in that room was laughing.

15
00:00:31,162 --> 00:00:33,207
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

16
00:00:33,207 --> 00:00:34,831
was inside that little box,

17
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:36,875
why Dottie had wrapped it so plain

18
00:00:36,875 --> 00:00:38,860
on purpose, and the letter at the

19
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:40,964
bottom that made the groom stand up

20
00:00:40,964 --> 00:00:42,889
in front of his whole family and

21
00:00:42,889 --> 00:00:45,174
say something to his mother he'd never

22
00:00:45,174 --> 00:00:45,896
said before.

23
00:00:46,076 --> 00:00:47,140
Stay with me,

24
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:47,631
honey.

25
00:00:47,811 --> 00:00:50,215
Dottie told me every word.

26
00:00:50,564 --> 00:00:53,208
Dottie and I have been friends for

27
00:00:53,208 --> 00:00:54,686
sixty years, honey.

28
00:00:54,866 --> 00:00:56,852
She and her husband Ray had a

29
00:00:56,852 --> 00:00:58,496
farm south of Mansfield,

30
00:00:58,496 --> 00:01:00,825
a hundred and twenty acres of corn

31
00:01:00,825 --> 00:01:03,497
and soybeans and an old white farmhouse

32
00:01:03,497 --> 00:01:05,141
with a wraparound porch.

33
00:01:05,321 --> 00:01:08,432
Ray's grandfather built it in nineteen ten.

34
00:01:08,612 --> 00:01:10,911
They raised three children there.

35
00:01:11,091 --> 00:01:13,929
Ray passed in two thousand nineteen,

36
00:01:13,929 --> 00:01:15,584
and Dottie stayed on,

37
00:01:15,584 --> 00:01:18,264
by herself, with the barn cats and

38
00:01:18,264 --> 00:01:19,682
a lot of memories.

39
00:01:20,032 --> 00:01:23,465
Her granddaughter Emily practically grew up on

40
00:01:23,465 --> 00:01:25,106
that farm, sweetheart.

41
00:01:25,286 --> 00:01:27,478
Every summer, she was there,

42
00:01:27,478 --> 00:01:29,905
riding on the tractor with Ray,

43
00:01:29,905 --> 00:01:33,350
collecting eggs, falling asleep on the porch

44
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:33,819
swing.

45
00:01:33,999 --> 00:01:35,919
When Emily was little,

46
00:01:35,919 --> 00:01:37,315
she used to say,

47
00:01:37,315 --> 00:01:39,410
Grandma, when I grow up,

48
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:42,115
I'm going to live here forever.

49
00:01:42,295 --> 00:01:44,766
And Dottie would laugh and say,

50
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:46,439
we'll see, sweet pea.

51
00:01:46,619 --> 00:01:48,187
We'll see.

52
00:01:48,367 --> 00:01:50,294
In a minute I'll tell you why

53
00:01:50,294 --> 00:01:52,221
that little promise mattered.

54
00:01:52,571 --> 00:01:55,232
Emily grew up and became a nurse,

55
00:01:55,232 --> 00:01:57,812
honey, and in twenty twenty-two,

56
00:01:57,812 --> 00:02:00,553
she married a young man named Josh

57
00:02:00,553 --> 00:02:01,279
Whitmore.

58
00:02:01,459 --> 00:02:02,732
A good boy,

59
00:02:02,732 --> 00:02:04,930
quiet, an engineer.

60
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:06,863
But his family had money.

61
00:02:07,043 --> 00:02:08,204
Real money.

62
00:02:08,384 --> 00:02:11,387
His father owned car dealerships all over

63
00:02:11,387 --> 00:02:13,583
northern Ohio, and his mother,

64
00:02:13,583 --> 00:02:16,073
Caroline, had a house on Lake Erie

65
00:02:16,073 --> 00:02:17,684
with eleven bathrooms.

66
00:02:17,864 --> 00:02:19,292
Dottie counted them.

67
00:02:19,472 --> 00:02:21,700
And from the very first day,

68
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:24,883
Caroline made it clear Emily wasn't good

69
00:02:24,883 --> 00:02:25,440
enough.

70
00:02:25,789 --> 00:02:27,949
Little things, sweetheart.

71
00:02:28,129 --> 00:02:30,150
At the engagement dinner,

72
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:33,464
Caroline asked Emily what her father did,

73
00:02:33,464 --> 00:02:35,889
and when Emily said he drove a

74
00:02:35,889 --> 00:02:37,586
truck, Caroline said,

75
00:02:37,586 --> 00:02:38,799
oh, how honest.

76
00:02:38,979 --> 00:02:39,986
At the wedding,

77
00:02:39,986 --> 00:02:42,202
she seated Dottie at the table by

78
00:02:42,202 --> 00:02:43,344
the kitchen door.

79
00:02:43,524 --> 00:02:45,307
And she called Emily,

80
00:02:45,307 --> 00:02:47,599
quote, Josh's little nurse.

81
00:02:47,779 --> 00:02:49,056
Not Emily.

82
00:02:49,236 --> 00:02:51,221
Josh's little nurse.

83
00:02:51,401 --> 00:02:53,724
Dottie heard it with her own ears,

84
00:02:53,724 --> 00:02:55,500
and she didn't say a word.

85
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:56,690
Not yet.

86
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:00,109
And Dottie noticed something else,

87
00:03:00,109 --> 00:03:00,650
honey.

88
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:03,105
Emily and Josh were living in an

89
00:03:03,105 --> 00:03:06,658
apartment over one of Josh's father's dealerships.

90
00:03:06,838 --> 00:03:07,988
Rent free.

91
00:03:08,168 --> 00:03:09,945
But Caroline had a key,

92
00:03:09,945 --> 00:03:12,649
and she came in whenever she liked.

93
00:03:12,829 --> 00:03:15,093
She rearranged Emily's kitchen.

94
00:03:15,273 --> 00:03:17,595
She threw out Emily's curtains.

95
00:03:17,775 --> 00:03:20,734
Emily called Dottie crying one night and

96
00:03:20,734 --> 00:03:23,250
said, Grandma, I don't feel like I

97
00:03:23,250 --> 00:03:24,137
have a home.

98
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:26,641
Dottie hung up the phone and sat

99
00:03:26,641 --> 00:03:28,892
in Ray's chair for a long time.

100
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:31,306
Dottie called me that week,

101
00:03:31,306 --> 00:03:33,676
honey, and asked me to come out

102
00:03:33,676 --> 00:03:34,594
to the farm.

103
00:03:34,774 --> 00:03:37,366
We sat at her kitchen table with

104
00:03:37,366 --> 00:03:38,743
the old oilcloth,

105
00:03:38,743 --> 00:03:39,796
and she said,

106
00:03:39,796 --> 00:03:41,740
Ruth, what would you do?

107
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:42,785
And I said,

108
00:03:42,785 --> 00:03:45,773
Dottie, I'd stand up at that Christmas

109
00:03:45,773 --> 00:03:48,369
party and give that woman a piece

110
00:03:48,369 --> 00:03:49,234
of my mind.

111
00:03:49,414 --> 00:03:51,626
And Dottie laughed and said,

112
00:03:51,626 --> 00:03:53,680
that's why I'm asking you,

113
00:03:53,680 --> 00:03:55,892
and then doing the opposite.

114
00:03:56,072 --> 00:03:58,252
I'll tell you what she did instead

115
00:03:58,252 --> 00:03:59,021
in a minute.

116
00:03:59,372 --> 00:04:02,138
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

117
00:04:02,138 --> 00:04:04,272
tap the like button for me,

118
00:04:04,272 --> 00:04:05,142
sweetheart.

119
00:04:05,322 --> 00:04:08,654
It helps more people find these stories.

120
00:04:08,834 --> 00:04:11,706
The Whitmores' Christmas party was a big

121
00:04:11,706 --> 00:04:12,711
affair, honey.

122
00:04:12,891 --> 00:04:15,162
Forty guests, a man in a tuxedo

123
00:04:15,162 --> 00:04:16,773
playing a white piano,

124
00:04:16,773 --> 00:04:19,117
a tree that touched the ceiling,

125
00:04:19,117 --> 00:04:22,340
and presents stacked up underneath in silver

126
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:24,757
paper from the department stores.

127
00:04:24,937 --> 00:04:27,423
Dottie came in her church dress and

128
00:04:27,423 --> 00:04:28,772
her good wool coat,

129
00:04:28,772 --> 00:04:31,045
with Ray's old watch on a ribbon

130
00:04:31,045 --> 00:04:32,181
around her neck.

131
00:04:32,532 --> 00:04:35,120
She'd wrapped her gift in brown paper

132
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,310
from a feed sack,

133
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:38,759
tied with kitchen twine and a sprig

134
00:04:38,759 --> 00:04:40,368
of cedar from the farm.

135
00:04:40,548 --> 00:04:42,476
On purpose, honey.

136
00:04:42,656 --> 00:04:44,103
She told me later,

137
00:04:44,103 --> 00:04:46,435
I wanted it to look like what

138
00:04:46,435 --> 00:04:46,998
it was.

139
00:04:47,178 --> 00:04:48,710
A piece of the farm.

140
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:51,046
And when she set it under that

141
00:04:51,046 --> 00:04:53,488
tree, among all that silver paper,

142
00:04:53,488 --> 00:04:56,075
Caroline picked it right back up and

143
00:04:56,075 --> 00:04:58,302
held it high for the whole room

144
00:04:58,302 --> 00:04:58,805
to see.

145
00:04:59,155 --> 00:05:00,532
And she said,

146
00:05:00,532 --> 00:05:02,122
in that bright,

147
00:05:02,122 --> 00:05:03,712
loud voice, oh,

148
00:05:03,712 --> 00:05:05,726
Dorothy, how sweet.

149
00:05:05,906 --> 00:05:08,191
Did you wrap it in a grocery

150
00:05:08,191 --> 00:05:08,518
bag?

151
00:05:08,698 --> 00:05:10,770
And forty people laughed,

152
00:05:10,770 --> 00:05:11,682
sweetheart.

153
00:05:11,862 --> 00:05:14,857
The man at the piano stopped playing.

154
00:05:15,037 --> 00:05:17,815
And Caroline put Dottie's present on the

155
00:05:17,815 --> 00:05:20,662
side table, with the children's gifts and

156
00:05:20,662 --> 00:05:22,677
the dog's Christmas stocking.

157
00:05:22,857 --> 00:05:25,724
And Emily's face went bright red.

158
00:05:25,904 --> 00:05:27,861
In a minute I'll tell you what

159
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:28,969
Dottie said back.

160
00:05:29,319 --> 00:05:31,549
Dottie didn't get angry,

161
00:05:31,549 --> 00:05:32,106
honey.

162
00:05:32,286 --> 00:05:34,662
She'd been a farm wife for fifty

163
00:05:34,662 --> 00:05:35,107
years.

164
00:05:35,287 --> 00:05:37,609
You don't get angry at hail.

165
00:05:37,789 --> 00:05:39,612
You just wait it out.

166
00:05:39,792 --> 00:05:41,289
She smiled and said,

167
00:05:41,289 --> 00:05:43,983
well, Caroline, it's what was handy.

168
00:05:44,163 --> 00:05:46,232
And she sat down in the corner

169
00:05:46,232 --> 00:05:47,611
with a cup of punch.

170
00:05:47,791 --> 00:05:49,382
But Josh had seen it.

171
00:05:49,562 --> 00:05:52,546
And Josh had seen his wife's face.

172
00:05:52,726 --> 00:05:54,781
And he didn't say anything.

173
00:05:54,961 --> 00:05:55,947
Not yet.

174
00:05:56,127 --> 00:05:58,368
He just went very quiet.

175
00:05:58,719 --> 00:06:01,145
So far, a farm built in nineteen

176
00:06:01,145 --> 00:06:03,344
ten, a little girl's promise,

177
00:06:03,344 --> 00:06:06,225
a mother-in-law with eleven bathrooms,

178
00:06:06,225 --> 00:06:08,423
and a brown paper box next to

179
00:06:08,423 --> 00:06:09,864
the dog's stocking.

180
00:06:10,044 --> 00:06:11,998
When the gifts were opened,

181
00:06:11,998 --> 00:06:13,445
late in the evening,

182
00:06:13,445 --> 00:06:15,327
everybody gathered around.

183
00:06:15,507 --> 00:06:18,073
Josh's father gave them a set of

184
00:06:18,073 --> 00:06:18,955
golf clubs.

185
00:06:19,135 --> 00:06:22,235
Caroline gave them a crystal punch bowl.

186
00:06:22,415 --> 00:06:24,226
All very fancy.

187
00:06:24,406 --> 00:06:25,753
Everyone clapped.

188
00:06:25,933 --> 00:06:28,373
And then Emily picked up the brown

189
00:06:28,373 --> 00:06:29,090
paper box.

190
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:30,494
Stay with me,

191
00:06:30,494 --> 00:06:31,058
honey.

192
00:06:31,409 --> 00:06:35,299
Caroline said, in that same bright voice,

193
00:06:35,299 --> 00:06:37,957
oh, look, Grandma's present.

194
00:06:38,137 --> 00:06:40,482
Let's see what the farm sent.

195
00:06:40,662 --> 00:06:42,833
And a few people chuckled again.

196
00:06:43,013 --> 00:06:45,630
Emily looked over at her grandmother in

197
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:46,368
the corner.

198
00:06:46,548 --> 00:06:48,649
And Dottie just nodded at her,

199
00:06:48,649 --> 00:06:50,681
once, the way she used to nod

200
00:06:50,681 --> 00:06:52,852
at Emily on the tractor when it

201
00:06:52,852 --> 00:06:55,023
was time to let out the clutch.

202
00:06:55,203 --> 00:06:57,560
Go ahead, sweet pea.

203
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:00,237
And Emily's hands were shaking a little

204
00:07:00,237 --> 00:07:01,966
as she pulled at the twine.

205
00:07:02,317 --> 00:07:04,979
She untied the twine and peeled back

206
00:07:04,979 --> 00:07:05,718
the paper.

207
00:07:05,898 --> 00:07:08,820
Inside was an old cigar box of

208
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:09,404
Ray's.

209
00:07:09,584 --> 00:07:12,235
And inside that was an old brass

210
00:07:12,235 --> 00:07:14,472
key, on a loop of red yarn.

211
00:07:14,652 --> 00:07:16,323
Emily knew that key,

212
00:07:16,323 --> 00:07:17,241
sweetheart.

213
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:19,422
It was the key to the front

214
00:07:19,422 --> 00:07:21,051
door of the farmhouse,

215
00:07:21,051 --> 00:07:23,644
the one Ray's grandfather had made.

216
00:07:23,824 --> 00:07:26,077
She'd used it a thousand times.

217
00:07:26,257 --> 00:07:27,783
And under the key,

218
00:07:27,783 --> 00:07:29,226
folded in thirds,

219
00:07:29,226 --> 00:07:31,601
was a stack of papers with a

220
00:07:31,601 --> 00:07:32,619
county seal.

221
00:07:32,969 --> 00:07:34,269
It was a deed,

222
00:07:34,269 --> 00:07:34,826
honey.

223
00:07:35,006 --> 00:07:37,495
Dottie had gone to her lawyer in

224
00:07:37,495 --> 00:07:38,118
October.

225
00:07:38,298 --> 00:07:41,385
She'd put the farmhouse and twenty acres

226
00:07:41,385 --> 00:07:43,006
around it, the house,

227
00:07:43,006 --> 00:07:44,704
the barn, the orchard,

228
00:07:44,704 --> 00:07:45,939
the porch swing,

229
00:07:45,939 --> 00:07:47,945
in Emily and Josh's names.

230
00:07:48,125 --> 00:07:50,130
And kept the rest of the land

231
00:07:50,130 --> 00:07:51,236
in her own name,

232
00:07:51,236 --> 00:07:54,070
to keep farming with her neighbor's boys.

233
00:07:54,250 --> 00:07:56,189
The room went silent.

234
00:07:56,369 --> 00:07:59,439
Caroline's punch bowl was still sitting there

235
00:07:59,439 --> 00:08:00,804
in its silver paper.

236
00:08:01,154 --> 00:08:03,246
Somebody near the piano,

237
00:08:03,246 --> 00:08:05,601
a man who sold real estate,

238
00:08:05,601 --> 00:08:09,176
said quietly, that's Route Thirteen land.

239
00:08:09,356 --> 00:08:11,527
That's worth more than this house.

240
00:08:11,707 --> 00:08:14,360
But that wasn't the part that mattered,

241
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:15,109
sweetheart.

242
00:08:15,289 --> 00:08:17,458
The part that mattered was at the

243
00:08:17,458 --> 00:08:19,364
very bottom of the cigar box.

244
00:08:19,544 --> 00:08:22,355
A letter, in Dottie's handwriting,

245
00:08:22,355 --> 00:08:23,596
on lined paper.

246
00:08:23,776 --> 00:08:25,834
And Emily read it out loud,

247
00:08:25,834 --> 00:08:28,044
because she couldn't help it,

248
00:08:28,044 --> 00:08:30,788
standing in the middle of that room.

249
00:08:31,139 --> 00:08:32,699
It said, Emily,

250
00:08:32,699 --> 00:08:33,739
sweet pea.

251
00:08:33,919 --> 00:08:35,243
When you were six,

252
00:08:35,243 --> 00:08:37,890
you told me you'd live here forever.

253
00:08:38,070 --> 00:08:39,823
I'm keeping my end.

254
00:08:40,003 --> 00:08:42,597
Every young wife needs one place in

255
00:08:42,597 --> 00:08:45,117
this world where nobody else has a

256
00:08:45,117 --> 00:08:45,413
key.

257
00:08:45,593 --> 00:08:46,882
This is yours.

258
00:08:47,062 --> 00:08:50,069
No one can rearrange your kitchen here.

259
00:08:50,249 --> 00:08:52,919
No one can throw out your curtains.

260
00:08:53,099 --> 00:08:55,613
Grandpa Ray would want you on that

261
00:08:55,613 --> 00:08:56,501
porch swing.

262
00:08:56,681 --> 00:08:58,802
Come home whenever you like,

263
00:08:58,802 --> 00:09:01,000
and stay as long as you want.

264
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:03,119
Love, Grandma.

265
00:09:03,469 --> 00:09:05,600
Emily got to the part about the

266
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:08,832
curtains, honey, and she couldn't read anymore.

267
00:09:09,012 --> 00:09:11,520
She just stood there and cried.

268
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:15,160
And everybody in that room understood exactly

269
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:16,545
what Dottie meant,

270
00:09:16,545 --> 00:09:19,544
because everybody in that room had seen

271
00:09:19,544 --> 00:09:22,158
Caroline throw those curtains out,

272
00:09:22,158 --> 00:09:23,542
or heard about it.

273
00:09:23,722 --> 00:09:25,615
Caroline went white.

274
00:09:25,795 --> 00:09:27,269
She opened her mouth.

275
00:09:27,449 --> 00:09:28,557
And her son,

276
00:09:28,557 --> 00:09:30,312
quiet, gentle Josh,

277
00:09:30,312 --> 00:09:33,175
stood up before she could say a

278
00:09:33,175 --> 00:09:33,637
word.

279
00:09:33,988 --> 00:09:35,427
He said, Mom.

280
00:09:35,607 --> 00:09:38,426
You laughed at Grandma Dottie's present in

281
00:09:38,426 --> 00:09:39,903
front of forty people.

282
00:09:40,083 --> 00:09:42,645
You called my wife my little nurse

283
00:09:42,645 --> 00:09:44,077
at our own wedding.

284
00:09:44,257 --> 00:09:46,879
You've let yourself into our home for

285
00:09:46,879 --> 00:09:47,658
six months.

286
00:09:47,838 --> 00:09:48,869
And I let you,

287
00:09:48,869 --> 00:09:51,078
because I didn't want a fight.

288
00:09:51,258 --> 00:09:52,349
That's on me.

289
00:09:52,529 --> 00:09:55,537
Then he took Emily's hand and said,

290
00:09:55,537 --> 00:09:56,999
it stops tonight.

291
00:09:57,179 --> 00:09:58,293
We're going home.

292
00:09:58,473 --> 00:10:00,774
And then he looked at the key

293
00:10:00,774 --> 00:10:02,281
and said, our home.

294
00:10:02,632 --> 00:10:04,835
And he went over to Dottie,

295
00:10:04,835 --> 00:10:08,181
sweetheart, in her corner with her punch,

296
00:10:08,181 --> 00:10:10,548
and he knelt down in front of

297
00:10:10,548 --> 00:10:12,344
her, in his good suit,

298
00:10:12,344 --> 00:10:13,323
and he said,

299
00:10:13,323 --> 00:10:13,649
Mrs.

300
00:10:13,829 --> 00:10:15,722
Dottie, thank you.

301
00:10:15,902 --> 00:10:18,758
I'm sorry I didn't say something sooner.

302
00:10:18,938 --> 00:10:22,113
And Dottie patted his cheek and said,

303
00:10:22,113 --> 00:10:24,516
you said it when it counted,

304
00:10:24,516 --> 00:10:24,859
son.

305
00:10:25,039 --> 00:10:27,529
Ray always said a man's measured by

306
00:10:27,529 --> 00:10:29,520
what he does when it counts.

307
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:31,921
They left the party together,

308
00:10:31,921 --> 00:10:33,300
the three of them,

309
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:34,449
before dessert.

310
00:10:34,799 --> 00:10:37,036
Dottie told me that on the drive

311
00:10:37,036 --> 00:10:40,114
home, honey, nobody said anything for twenty

312
00:10:40,114 --> 00:10:40,673
minutes.

313
00:10:40,853 --> 00:10:43,269
Then Emily, in the back seat,

314
00:10:43,269 --> 00:10:44,684
started to laugh.

315
00:10:44,864 --> 00:10:46,861
And Josh started to laugh.

316
00:10:47,041 --> 00:10:48,922
And Dottie started to laugh.

317
00:10:49,102 --> 00:10:51,366
And they laughed all the way down

318
00:10:51,366 --> 00:10:53,218
Route Thirteen in the snow,

319
00:10:53,218 --> 00:10:55,275
until Dottie had to pull over,

320
00:10:55,275 --> 00:10:58,087
because she couldn't see the road through

321
00:10:58,087 --> 00:10:58,773
her tears.

322
00:10:58,953 --> 00:11:01,084
Josh said, Grandma Dottie,

323
00:11:01,084 --> 00:11:03,297
did you just give us a farm

324
00:11:03,297 --> 00:11:04,526
in a feed sack?

325
00:11:04,876 --> 00:11:07,786
Emily and Josh moved into that farmhouse

326
00:11:07,786 --> 00:11:08,951
in March, honey.

327
00:11:09,131 --> 00:11:11,859
Josh fixed the porch roof himself.

328
00:11:12,039 --> 00:11:15,161
Emily painted the kitchen yellow and hung

329
00:11:15,161 --> 00:11:18,358
curtains she sewed from Dottie's old flour

330
00:11:18,358 --> 00:11:19,272
sack fabric.

331
00:11:19,452 --> 00:11:21,872
And Dottie moved into the little apartment

332
00:11:21,872 --> 00:11:22,794
over the garage,

333
00:11:22,794 --> 00:11:24,580
so she could have her coffee on

334
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:27,289
the porch every morning with her granddaughter.

335
00:11:27,469 --> 00:11:30,035
Last summer, they had a baby girl.

336
00:11:30,215 --> 00:11:32,045
They named her Raye,

337
00:11:32,045 --> 00:11:33,326
after Grandpa.

338
00:11:33,676 --> 00:11:35,011
And Caroline?

339
00:11:35,191 --> 00:11:37,296
It took her almost a year,

340
00:11:37,296 --> 00:11:38,186
sweetheart.

341
00:11:38,366 --> 00:11:39,636
But last Christmas,

342
00:11:39,636 --> 00:11:41,575
she drove down from the lake,

343
00:11:41,575 --> 00:11:44,183
and she knocked on that farmhouse door.

344
00:11:44,363 --> 00:11:45,942
She knocked, honey.

345
00:11:46,122 --> 00:11:47,631
She didn't have a key.

346
00:11:47,811 --> 00:11:49,890
And when Emily opened it,

347
00:11:49,890 --> 00:11:52,967
Caroline was holding a box wrapped in

348
00:11:52,967 --> 00:11:55,961
plain brown paper and kitchen twine.

349
00:11:56,141 --> 00:11:58,544
Inside was a baby quilt.

350
00:11:58,724 --> 00:12:00,525
And a note that said,

351
00:12:00,525 --> 00:12:01,383
I'm sorry.

352
00:12:01,563 --> 00:12:02,736
I'm learning.

353
00:12:03,086 --> 00:12:05,702
Here's what I want you to remember,

354
00:12:05,702 --> 00:12:06,151
honey.

355
00:12:06,331 --> 00:12:08,827
Don't judge a gift by its paper.

356
00:12:09,007 --> 00:12:11,494
And don't judge a grandmother by her

357
00:12:11,494 --> 00:12:11,840
coat.

358
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:13,720
Tell me in the comments,

359
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:16,909
sweetheart, what's the most precious gift you

360
00:12:16,909 --> 00:12:19,531
ever got that came in plain wrapping?

361
00:12:19,711 --> 00:12:21,859
There are more of my family's stories

362
00:12:21,859 --> 00:12:23,891
in the playlist right there on your

363
00:12:23,891 --> 00:12:24,297
screen.

364
00:12:24,477 --> 00:12:25,383
Good night.

365
00:12:25,563 --> 00:12:26,909
Merry Christmas.
